DCE/RPC runtime
===============
This is an example of a full DCE/RPC runtime that allows building DCE/RPC
services as standalone services that runs as separate processes.

It contains a master DCE/RPC daemon that contains a built-in EPM.
This server also provides a unix domain socket where separate standalone
services can connect and register a named pipe with the master daemon.
Once registered, the pipe is reachable in IPC$ on the master server.

The master daemon only does SMB2: it forwards what a client writes to the
pipe to the service, and passes what the service sends back to the
client's reads. All of the DCE/RPC (BIND, auth, fragments, NDR32/NDR64 in
either byte order) runs inside the service, with libdcerpc's server
runtime (<dcerpc/server.h>), so one pipe can serve any number of
interfaces, e.g. lsarpc and dssetup on \pipe\lsarpc. The interfaces a
service lists when it registers are advertised in the built-in EPM.
The control protocol is described in external.c and dcerpcd_proto.h.

It has boilerplate and tooling to build completely standalone services
running as separate processes and which can be started, stopped at runtime.

axpy is an example service that implements a new protocol "AXPY" as a
standalone service.

IDL files can be compiled into a shared library containing the coders for
all the types in the idl and then loaded into the libdcerpc
"./utils/dcerpc" tool. This allows the dcerpc tool to parse and understand
YAML descriptions of requests for that protocol and send to the service.

Example:
./utils/dcerpc -c dcerpcd/axpy/axpy_coder.so \
    'smb://127.0.0.1:4455/IPC$/axpy?sec=ntlmssp' <<EOF
compute: Request
  x: 5
  y: 7
EOF

YAML:
---
compute: Response
  y: 17
  result: The result is: 17
  Status: 0   



Authentication
--------------
NTLM and Kereberos is supported.

For NTLM the usernames must map to a local user with the same name.

For kerberos, the incoming SPN, of the form <username>/fqdn@REALM
is mapped straight to <username> locally.

Other mappings are planned for later.


idl-compiler
------------
dcerpcd uses libdcerpc's python based idl-compiler, ../idlcomp.py.
This compiler can compile a fairly large subset of DCE/RPC, at least
as much as is required to compile virtually all interfaces that MS uses
for their services so it should be complete enough for your use-cases too.
If not, let me know and I will expand it.

The compiler generates coders (encoders/decoders) for the data structures
and supports NDR/YAML/JSON as the encoding format.

NDR is used for the PDUs exchanged with a client, while the other two encodings
are useful if you want to write data to a local file to later read back.
Those usecase could be configuration files or just any kind of data store
where you want it to be human readable and human editable.

Some protocols carry an encoded structure inside a plain byte array, for
example a self-relative security descriptor in srvsvc SHARE_INFO_502. The
blob() attribute tells idlcomp what such a byte array holds:

  uint32_t reserved;
  [size_is(reserved),blob(SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR)] uint8_t *security_descriptor;

The NDR wire format is unchanged (length + unique pointer to the bytes), but
the C field becomes a SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR *, the length is filled in on
encode, and YAML/JSON show the decoded structure instead of raw bytes (the
length field is omitted there). The types blob() accepts are listed in
BLOB_TYPES in idlcomp.py; any libdcerpc type with a coder can be added there.

dcerpcd itself uses two data-only IDL files for its local YAML files:

  config.idl   → CONFIG root (port, krb5, keytab, hostname, domain)
  passwd.idl   → PASSWD root (count + entry[] of user/password)

They are compiled by the Makefile into config_coder.c / passwd_coder.c and
loaded at runtime with dcerpc_read_yaml_file(). Example shapes:

  CONFIG:
    port: 445
    krb5: 1
    keytab: /etc/krb5.keytab

  PASSWD:
    count: 1
    entry:
      - user: alice
        password: secret

Full-line and trailing # comments are allowed in these YAML files.
The passwd file must be mode 0600 and owned by the process euid.

If the idl contains an endpoint description block, like this:
[
  uuid(d5d385d6-90b4-421b-89fb-5a08ca755dee),
  version(1.0),
  helpstring("A * X plus Y"),
  endpoint("ncacn_np:[\\pipe\\axpy]"),
  pointer_default(unique)
]

Then the idl compiler will also generate the strings serviced.c needs to
register the pipe with the master dcerpcd server, and a stub.c where you
can fill in what the actual functions should do.


Access control
--------------
If you want access control based on the user that sent the request
you have to run the service as root. In that case the service will
switch to the uid that the authenticated user mapped to locally
while it handles what that user wrote to the pipe, stubs included.


services
--------
Services are generated mostly automatically by the idl compiler.
See axpy/ and axpy/README for an example service.

axpy/serviced.c is a generic service: built with -DSERVICE=axpy it serves
the axpy interface on \pipe\axpy. To serve several interfaces on one
pipe, name the pipe and list them:

  -DSERVICE=lsarpc -DSERVICE_INTERFACES='X(lsa) X(dssetup)'

and link each interface's coder and stubs.


libdcerpc interfaces
--------------------
The Windows interfaces (srvsvc, wkssvc, winreg, lsa, epm) are client-side
coders in libdcerpc, generated from ../<iface>/<iface>.idl. dcerpcd
serves only its endpoint mapper (with libdcerpc's epm coders: ept_lookup,
ept_map and ept_lookup_handle_free) and forwards the pipes of the external
services registered with it.
